ly reviewed on both its UK publication (February 2018, Raven Books) and its US publication (September 2018) and received an exceptionally warm critical reception for a debut. The dominant critical vocabulary centres on: **ingenuity**, **complexity**, **formal daring**, and the pleasures and occasional frustrations of its puzzle-box architecture.\n\n#### Key Lines of Praise\n\n- **Formal originality:** Reviewers almost universally praised the audacity of yoking the \"Golden Age country house mystery\" (Christie, Marsh, Sayers) to the time-loop mechanism. The *Guardian*, *The Times*, and *The Sunday Times* all highlighted this as the novel's distinguishing achievement. Critics noted that the time-loop is not merely decorative — it is structurally load-bearing, because the mystery is literally unsolvable from any single perspective. Each host provides information inaccessible to the others.\n\n- **The multiple-perspective mechanism:** Critical commentary frequently focused on how Turton's device inverts the classic mystery's epistemological contract. In a conventional Christie, the detective (and by proxy the reader) gradually approaches a single authoritative truth. Here, because each host body has different social relationships, different mobility, different prejudices, and different physical capabilities, the reader must hold eight partly unreliable perspective-sets in mind simultaneously. Reviewers in outlets like *NPR Books* and *Publishers Weekly* compared this to assembling a mosaic where each tile is individually distorted.\n\n- **The \"unreliable narrator by design\" problem:** Several critics noted the novel raises interesting questions about what \"reliability\" means when unreliability is not a character flaw but an ontological condition — Bishop is not lying or self-deceiving; he simply cannot *be everywhere at once*, and each host filters experience differently. This was identified as a genuinely new wrinkle on the classic \"unreliable narrator\" device.\n\n- **Plotting complexity:** Many reviewers acknowledged that the novel is extraordinarily dense and that readers who try to track every clue will find it rewarding on re-read. A few reviewers found the middle sections demanding or occasionally confusing — a criticism that appeared in some trade reviews (the *Kirkus* starred review reportedly noted the complexity while praising the execution; *Publishers Weekly* gave it a starred review as well).\n\n#### Critical Reservations\nA minority of reviews noted:\n- The emotional stakes can feel subordinated to the intellectual puzzle; character interiority is harder to sustain when the protagonist keeps changing bodies.\n- Some readers found the sheer density of the plot mechanics occasionally overwhelming.\n- The ending's revelations require the reader to trust that Turton has played entirely fair — some critics debated whether all clues were sufficiently signposted.\n\n#### Awards Context\nThe Costa First Novel Award win gave the book significant critical cachet in the UK. Judges' comments emphasised the novel's structural innovation and the confidence of the plotting as exceptional for a debut.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Scholarly and Academic Analysis\n\nAs of my knowledge cutoff, *dedicated peer-reviewed academic articles* focused specifically on *The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle* are sparse — which is not unusual for a novel only six years old at the time of writing, especially one sitting at the popular/literary boundary. However, the novel has begun appearing in the following scholarly contexts:\n\n#### Genre Studies and Neovictorian/Golden Age Revival\nScholars working on the contemporary resurgence of the Golden Age mystery form — writers like Sophie Hannah (the authorised Poirot continuations), Anthony Horowitz, and Richard Osman alongside Turton — have used the novel as a case study in **genre self-consciousness**. The argument is that Turton is not simply writing a Christie pastiche but foregrounding the *machiner